+## Foreign canaries (SOV-015)
+With `[foreign] enabled = true`, an own canary's twin C′ also goes to the registered nodes of the
+other gateways (every one, or a random `fanout` of them), and primed learns which ones:
+`POST /sovereignty/canary {..., "foreign": [G2, G3, ...]}`. Those nodes hold C′ and reject C when it
+relays in (C′ pays 1 sat more than C, far under the replacement bump, and C never signals RBF), so an
+honest G2 never templates C. A G2 whose checked job carries C builds from a source that got C, the
+same source as G's node; primed joins such gateways into a `shared-template-source` cluster and
+splits one builder's independence across it (`prime/tides/src/entropy.rs`, `shared_sources`).
+
+Order within one own canary:
+1. `prioritisetransaction C 0 -1e8` on the pool node;
+2. open and handshake the P2P connection to G's node **and** to every target, in parallel (an
+   unreachable target is dropped, the canary still goes ahead);
+3. `order = "own-ack"` (default): push C to G's node and wait for its `pong`, then push C′ on every
+   target connection at once; `order = "pipelined"`: push C and every C′ back to back, then collect
+   the pongs;
+4. `sendrawtransaction C′` on the pool node.
+
+A target is reported only if its `pong` for C′ came back within `max_lag_secs` (0.05) of C being
+sent: the window in which C, relayed out of G's node, could reach it first. G's node announces C
+after a random trickle (Poisson, mean 2 s to its outbound peers, 5 s to its inbound ones); a node
+that G's node connected to also waits 2 s before it asks an inbound announcer for the tx. So the
+worst case is a target that connected to G's node: the race is lost with probability about
+`lag / 5 s`, ≈ 1% at 50 ms. Measured on Knots 29.4.2 (SOV-015 `race_probe.py`, 40 trials per lag):
+0/80 at ≤ 0.1 s for both kinds of peer; 3/40 at 0.25 s and 5/40 at 1 s for a node that connected to
+G's node; 0/40 up to 2 s for a node G's node connected to. The sidecar's loopback fan-out acks in ≈ 1
+ms. `own-ack` makes one node behind several addresses (the SOV-015 farm) take C before its own
+twin; `pipelined` is for slow links (Tor), where the extra round trip would push every twin past
+`max_lag_secs`.
+
+Cost: no fee (only one of C/C′ confirms, and C′ only 1 sat more), one extra P2P connection per
+target per own canary (`n·k` per round for `n` due gateways and fan-out `k`), and each target's node
+learns one more canary twin. `status` counts `foreign.twins`, `in_time`, `late`, `failed`; the
+ledger's `placed` record carries `foreign` and every target's `foreign_lags`.
+
